as you look to make your services more  reliable and easier to operate  logging becomes increasingly important  both during normal operation  and troubleshooting today i'm here to  show you what's new in google cloud  logging  and help you get started this is the  stackdoctor  [Music]  we've looked at logging errors in the  previous episode but cloud logging has  gotten a major update since then  the logs viewer is entirely new and  there are new capabilities under the  hood let's take a look  you can access the logs in your cloud  project by going to the products menu  and selecting logging under operations  this will bring you to the logs viewer  note that you can pin products in the  menu so that they're always at the top  of the list  logging is probably one of the ones i  use most  the new logs viewer has three main  sections the query builder at the top  is what you use to filter the logs in  your project to just the entries you  need  the query results at the bottom shows  you all the log entries that match your  query  the histogram view in the middle shows  you the volume of logs that match your  query  over time this is incredibly useful if  for example you're looking to understand  when a particular error started  happening  there are two primary ways to create  queries against your logs  the first one is use the menu selections  the second is to write the query  directly  let's take a look at both first you can  use the menu selection in the query  builder to select the resource  log type and log entries severity that  you'd like to include in your query  you can make your selections as granular  or as broad as you like  the query builder will then generate the  string query for you based on your  selections  your second option is to write the query  directly without using the menu  selectors  this may become easier and faster for  you once you spend some time using the  query builder  we've also published a library of  queries you can use to get started we'll  link to that below  note that this new logs viewer is still  in a preview stage this means that lots  of features are still being developed  one of these that's only available in  the classic logs viewer is the ability  to save and share queries  this is incredibly valuable when working  with teammates  you've now seen how to view and query  logs in your project but how do logs  actually get there  let's take a look under the hood the  cloud logging api  is the external endpoint that serves as  the single entry point for sending logs  to cloud logging  no matter where logs are coming from  whether it's from a logging agent  installed on compute vm  your application running in gke or app  engine or any of the google cloud  services  they're all sent to the logging api the  api  sends log entries to log router this is  the internal plumbing that determines  what happens to log entries that it  receives  the log router evaluates each entry to  determine whether it meets the filter  criteria for any exclusions  if an exclusion is applicable that entry  is discarded  that's not the case the entry is stored  in the cloud logging back-end and is  then available to be query through the  logs viewer or for log-based metrics  the log router also evaluates each entry  that it receives  to determine whether it should be  exported to a downstream destination  you can configure exports to cloud  storage for archiving bigquery for  analytics  or pub stuff to send logs to third-party  or event processing systems  note that the exclusion and export  evaluations happen in parallel on the  same log entry  that means you can both export log  entries and exclude them  this can often be used to great effect  to save costs  thanks for joining me today we looked at  the new logs viewer and learn how to  build log queries  we also took a look under the hood and  learned about how the log router works  to allow you to precisely control which  logs are stored  discarded or exported that's it for this  episode come back soon for more  this is the stack doctor stay healthy  out there  [Music]
 

